\title{A Combinatorial Theory of Anti-Gravity Theorems:\\ Weight, Cheapness, and the Structure of Dependency}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We introduce a finite combinatorial model of mathematical libraries in which each theorem is a vertex of a directed \emph{dependency graph} and an edge $a \to b$ records that theorem $b$ uses theorem $a$ in its proof. Within this model we define the \emph{gravitational weight} of a theorem as the number of theorems that depend on it, and we call a theorem \emph{anti-gravity} when it combines \emph{high weight} with a \emph{short proof}. We prove a coherent collection of results: a handshake identity equating total weight with total dependency count; tight upper bounds on weight; an averaging principle guaranteeing a heaviest theorem carrying at least the average load; a pigeonhole existence theorem giving precise conditions under which an anti-gravity theorem must exist; a monotonicity theorem showing that, under transitive dependency, more foundational theorems are heavier; and two families of fully explicit witnesses --- a \emph{linear} library realizing weight $\Theta(n)$ and a \emph{grid} library realizing weight $\Theta(n\cdot m)$, each with constant proof length. Finally we refute, by explicit counterexample, the folklore universal claims that anti-gravity theorems appear in \emph{every} library and that a fixed fraction of theorems are anti-gravity: a dependency-free library contains none.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Mathematical folklore holds that a handful of theorems in any subject do a disproportionate share of the work: they have short, memorable proofs yet appear in the proofs of a vast number of downstream results. The Fundamental Theorem of Algebra, once complex analysis is available, is proved in a few lines but underwrites an enormous body of algebra. We make the phenomenon precise, calling such theorems \emph{anti-gravity} --- echoing the physical fantasy of a device providing great lift for negligible effort --- because they provide great mathematical \emph{support} (many dependents) for negligible \emph{cost} (a short proof).

Our contributions are: a minimal finite model (Section~\ref{sec:model}); structural identities and bounds (Section~\ref{sec:bounds}); an averaging principle and pigeonhole existence theorem (Section~\ref{sec:existence}); a monotonicity theorem under transitive dependency (Section~\ref{sec:foundations}); explicit linear and quadratic witnesses (Section~\ref{sec:witnesses}); and a refutation of the over-strong universal predictions (Section~\ref{sec:limits}). We separate throughout what is \emph{provable} from what is \emph{folklore}.

\section{The model}\label{sec:model}

\begin{definition}[Library]
A \emph{library} is a finite type $V$ (the theorems) with a decidable binary relation $D$ on $V$. We read $D(a,b)$ as ``$b$ depends on $a$'', i.e.\ theorem $a$ is used in the proof of theorem $b$. Write $N = |V|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Gravitational weight]
The \emph{gravitational weight} of $a \in V$ is the number of theorems depending on it,
\[ \wt(a) = \#\{\, b \in V : D(a,b) \,\}. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[In-degree]
The \emph{in-degree} of $b \in V$ is $\indeg(b) = \#\{\, a \in V : D(a,b) \,\}$, the number of theorems it directly depends on.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Proof length]
A \emph{proof-length function} is any $\ell : V \to \mathbb{N}$; $\ell(a)$ measures the cost of the proof of $a$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Anti-gravity theorem]
Fix a weight threshold $w_0 \in \mathbb{N}$, a length bound $\ell_0 \in \mathbb{N}$, and a proof-length function $\ell$. A theorem $a$ is \emph{anti-gravity at $(w_0,\ell_0)$} if
\[ \wt(a) \ge w_0 \qquad\text{and}\qquad \ell(a) \le \ell_0. \]
\end{definition}

These definitions assume no acyclicity, connectivity, or metric on statements; every result below holds at this generality unless a hypothesis is stated.

\section{Conservation and bounds}\label{sec:bounds}

\begin{theorem}[Handshake identity]
For every library, $\displaystyle \sum_{a \in V} \wt(a) = \sum_{b \in V} \indeg(b).$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both sides count the set of dependency pairs $\{(a,b) : D(a,b)\}$. Writing $\wt(a) = \sum_b [D(a,b)]$ and $\indeg(b) = \sum_a [D(a,b)]$, the two double sums differ only by the order of summation, so they agree by commuting finite sums.
\end{proof}

The identity is a conservation law: total \emph{support} equals total \emph{reliance}; each edge is counted once from each end.

\begin{theorem}[Weight ceiling]
For every $a \in V$, $\wt(a) \le N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The dependents of $a$ form a subset of the $N$-element set $V$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict ceiling under irreflexivity]
If $V$ is nonempty and $D$ is irreflexive ($\neg D(a,a)$ for all $a$), then $\wt(a) < N$ for every $a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Irreflexivity means $a$ is not among its own dependents, so the dependent set is a proper subset of $V$, hence of strictly smaller cardinality.
\end{proof}

\section{Averaging and existence}\label{sec:existence}

\begin{theorem}[A heaviest theorem exists]
If $V$ is nonempty, there is $a^\star \in V$ with $\wt(b) \le \wt(a^\star)$ for all $b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An integer-valued function on a nonempty finite set attains its maximum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Averaging bound]
If $a^\star$ maximizes weight, then $\displaystyle \sum_{b \in V} \wt(b) \le N \cdot \wt(a^\star)$; equivalently $\wt(a^\star)$ is at least the average weight.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Bound each summand by the maximum and sum over the $N$ theorems.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of anti-gravity theorems]\label{thm:existence}
Let $S = \{\, a \in V : \ell(a) \le \ell_0 \,\}$ be nonempty. If
\[ |S| \cdot w_0 \le \sum_{a \in S} \wt(a), \]
then some $a \in S$ is anti-gravity at $(w_0,\ell_0)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $a$ maximize $\wt$ over the nonempty finite set $S$; then $a$ has a short proof. If $a$ were not anti-gravity, $\wt(a) < w_0$, and by maximality $\wt(b) < w_0$ for all $b \in S$; summing over $|S|$ elements gives $\sum_{a\in S}\wt(a) < |S| \cdot w_0$, contradicting the hypothesis. Hence $\wt(a) \ge w_0$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:existence} is the precise rendering of ``anti-gravity theorems exist'': existence is forced exactly when the cheap theorems collectively carry at least $w_0$ weight on average.

\section{Foundations are heaviest}\label{sec:foundations}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity under transitive dependency]
If $D$ is transitive, then $D(a,b)$ implies $\wt(b) \le \wt(a)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $D(a,b)$ and $c$ is a dependent of $b$ (so $D(b,c)$), transitivity gives $D(a,c)$, so $c$ depends on $a$. Thus the dependents of $b$ inject into those of $a$, and cardinalities give the bound.
\end{proof}

Along a transitive dependency order, weight increases toward the foundations. Since foundational results tend to have the shortest proofs, this is the structural reason anti-gravity clusters at the base of a subject.

\section{Explicit witnesses}\label{sec:witnesses}

\subsection{The linear library}

\begin{construction}
For $n \ge 1$, let $V = \{0,\dots,n-1\}$ with $D(i,j) \iff i < j$.
\end{construction}

\begin{theorem}[Bottom weight, linear case]
The bottom theorem $0$ has weight $\wt(0) = n-1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The dependents of $0$ are exactly $1,\dots,n-1$: the universe minus $\{0\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Linear anti-gravity witness]
For $n \ge 1$ and $\ell \equiv 1$, the bottom theorem $0$ is anti-gravity at $(n-1, 1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\wt(0) = n-1 \ge n-1$ and $\ell(0) = 1 \le 1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The grid library}

\begin{construction}
For $n,m \ge 1$, let $V = \{0,\dots,n-1\}\times\{0,\dots,m-1\}$ with $D(p,q) \iff p_{\mathrm{row}} < q_{\mathrm{row}}$.
\end{construction}

\begin{theorem}[Bottom-row weight, grid case]
A bottom-row node $p$ has weight $\wt(p) = (n-1)\cdot m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Its dependents are all nodes in rows $1,\dots,n-1$: $n-1$ rows of $m$ nodes each.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quadratic anti-gravity witness]
With $\ell \equiv 1$, a bottom-row node is anti-gravity at $((n-1)\cdot m,\, 1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Its weight is $(n-1)\cdot m$ and its proof length is $1$.
\end{proof}

Taking $m = \Theta(n)$ yields weight $\Theta(n^2)$ with constant proof length: one lemma underwriting a quadratic family of consequences.

\section{The limits of universality}\label{sec:limits}

\begin{theorem}[No dependencies, no anti-gravity]\label{thm:refute}
Let $V$ carry the empty dependency relation $D \equiv \bot$. Then for every $w_0 \ge 1$, every $\ell$, and every $\ell_0$, there is no anti-gravity theorem.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With the empty relation $\wt(a) = 0$ for all $a$, but anti-gravity requires $\wt(a) \ge w_0 \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The claims ``anti-gravity theorems exist in every library'' and ``a fixed positive fraction of theorems in any library are anti-gravity'' are both false; Theorem~\ref{thm:refute} gives a fraction of exactly $0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[On ``density in the space of all theorems'']
This is underspecified rather than false: there is no canonical topology on ``the space of all theorems.'' Under the discrete topology it is vacuous; under an edit-distance metric on statements it is a genuine but model-dependent question. We do not assert it as a theorem.
\end{remark}

\section{Discussion}

Anti-gravity is the joint occurrence of two orthogonal quantities: \emph{weight} (a global network property) and \emph{cheapness} (a local per-proof property). The theory shows existence is conditional (Theorem~\ref{thm:existence}), weight concentrates at the foundations (Section~\ref{sec:foundations}), and the phenomenon is tunable to any linear or quadratic profile (Section~\ref{sec:witnesses}), while being honest about the absence of any universal positive fraction (Section~\ref{sec:limits}).

Applications include prioritizing verification in large corpora (high-weight, short-proof theorems are the highest-leverage audit targets), explaining exposition and curriculum design, and providing global health metrics for library refactoring.

\section{Future work}

Systematic study of weight under reflexive-transitive closure; the distribution of anti-gravity theorems in random dependency graphs; a defensible topology/metric for the density question; and a continuous cost measure with its (weight, cost) Pareto frontier.

\section{Conclusion}

We gave a compact, self-contained theory of anti-gravity theorems: definitions, a conservation identity, sharp bounds, an averaging principle, a pigeonhole existence theorem, a monotonicity theorem, explicit linear and quadratic witnesses, and an honest refutation of the over-strong universal claims. The dream of enormous lift for negligible effort is impossible in physics; in mathematics it is commonplace --- and now precisely characterized.

\end{document}

